Cg a49.750000,50.000000,365
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Times New Roman;}
{\f2\fnil Symbol;}
{\f3\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We begin our study of DSP systems
 with a discussion of models for their signals.
 A signal will be defined as a sequence 
whose index corresponds to a time instant.
 }{\i0 \b0 \ulnone A sequence is represented
 in }{a computer by an indexed array using
 a programming language. We show how this
 is done in Mathcad and go on to implement
 basic operations on signals.}}
}
.TXT 15 0 239 0
Cg a50.375000,50.000000,648
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Times New Roman;}
{\f2\fnil Symbol;}
{\f3\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\fs20 \b0 \i0 \i0 \b0 A }{\fs20 
\b0 \i0 \b \i0 digital signal}{\fs20 \b0 
\i0 \i0 \b0  is a real or complex valued
 function of an integer variable n, in other
 words, a }{\b \fs20 \i0 \i0 sequence}{\fs20 
\b0 \i0 .}{\fs20 \b0 \i0 \i0 \b0  The index
 n will be usually interpreted as }{\fs20 
\b0 \i0 \b \i0 discrete time}{\fs20 \b0 
\i0 \b0 \i0 . A digital signal is denoted
 \{s}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 \i0 n}{\fs20 \b0 \i0 
\} or, more casually, by s}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 
\i0 n}{\fs20 \b0 \i0 . For digital signals
 of length N, the index n conventionally
 }{takes on the values \{0,1,..., N-1\}.
 We shall be occupied with signals of finite
 length for some time. }}
}
.TXT 16 0 1 0
Cg a46.875000,50.250000,321
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Times New Roman;}
{\f2\fnil Symbol;}
{\f3\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\ulnone \b \i \i0 \b0 \b0 We define
 a }{\b \i0 \ulnone length}{\ulnone \b \i 
\b0 \i0 , }{\ulnone \b \i \i0 \b0 \b0 N}{\ulnone 
\b \i \i0 \b0 \b0 , }{\ulnone \b \i \i0 
\b0 \b0 and }{\b \i0 \ulnone discrete time
 index}{\ulnone \b \i \b0 \i0 , }{\ulnone 
\b \i \i0 \b0 \b0 n}{\ulnone \b \i \i0 \b0 
\b0 , }{\ulnone \b \i \i0 \b0 \b0 for a 
signal sequence. }}
}
