{\plain {\fs24 \f1 \b \ulnone Example 1.2
 ______________________________________}{\b 
\f0  \par }{\f0 \b0 We }{\f0 \b0 \f0 \b0 
next }{\f0 \b0 define a }{\b \f0 \i0 sinusoidal}{\b 
\f0  sequence}{\f0 \b0 . A s}{\b0 \f0 inusoidal
 sequence}{\f0 \b0  is of the form }{\f0 
\b0 \f0 \b0 cos(}{\f0 \b0 \f2 \b0 w}{\f0 
\b0 \f0 \b0 n+}{\f2 \b0 q}{\f0 \b0 )}{\f0 
\b0  with}{\b \f0  frequency}{\f0 \b0  }{\f2 
\b0 w}{ and }{\b phase}{ }{\f2 q}{. Notice
 immediately that, for an integer k,  cos((}{\f2 
w}{+2}{\f2 p}{k)n+}{\f2 q}{) = }{\f0 \b0 
cos(}{\f2 \b0 w}{\f0 \b0 n+}{\f2 \b0 q}{\f0 
\b0 ) because the cosine function has period
 2}{\f2 \b0 p}{\f0 \b0 . Therefore, the 
frequencies \{}{\f2 \b0 w}{\f0 \b0 +k2}{\f2 
\b0 p}{\f0 \b0 , k = 0, +/-1,...\} are }{\ul 
\f0 \b0 indistinguishable}{\f0 \b0 : they
 are said to be }{\b \f0 aliases}{\f0 \b0 
. We will see that it is convenient to express
 the frequency in a particular form. }}
}
