}
{\plain {But, as }{\f2 w}{ is not distinguishable
 from its aliases, we see that all the analog
 frequencies \{}{\f2 n}{ + k 2}{\f2 p}{/Ts,
 k = 0,+/-1,...\} are indistinguishable-
 i.e., aliases. }{The }{\b sampling frequency
 }{\b0 is }{\b0 \f2 w}{\b0 \fs16 \dn6 s}{\b0 
 = 2}{\b0 \f2 p}{\b0 /T}{\b0 \fs16 \dn6 
s}{\b0  }{\b0 . We shall later study this
 aliasing phenomenon for general analog 
signals in }{\ul \b Chapter 15}{\b0 .}}
}
